# Paths that must never be tracked in this repo.
#
# One extended-regex per line, matched against paths relative to the repo
# root. Blank lines and #-comments ignored.
#
# Read by BOTH .githooks/pre-commit and the "No secret files are tracked" CI
# step, so the local guard and the remote one cannot drift apart. Adding a
# pattern here arms both at once — the same reason deps.conf is one file.

# The machine-local pair. These live in $HOME, outside the work tree, so this
# is a backstop for someone recreating them in the repo, not the main defence.
#
# The optional leading dot is the whole point of the backstop and was missing.
# In $HOME these files are `.bash_local` and `.bash_password_commands`, so the
# realistic way one lands in the repo is `cp ~/.bash_local .` — which keeps the
# dot, and which the un-dotted pattern sailed straight past. The bare names are
# kept because bash/bash_local is the shape the repo's own naming would produce.
(^|/)\.?(bash_local|bash_password_commands)$

# Environment files
(^|/)\.env(\..+)?$

# Key material and keystores
\.(pem|pfx|p12|keystore|jks)$
(^|/)\.?id_(rsa|dsa|ecdsa|ed25519)$

# Credential files that tools write into $HOME and people paste into repos.
#
# _netrc is not a typo: it is what curl and git read on Windows, where a
# leading dot is awkward to create in Explorer. This repo ships a PowerShell
# installer and a second clone on C:, so the Windows spelling of a credentials
# file is as reachable here as the POSIX one.
(^|/)(\.|_)netrc$
(^|/)\.pypirc$
(^|/)\.npmrc$
